   "text": "# portlog\n\nKnow what held a port—even after the process exits.\n\nportlog records which process held each local TCP port over time, so you can answer “what was on port 3000 an hour ago?”\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nRuns on macOS and Linux.\n\n## Start recording\n\n```sh\nportlog start\n```\n\nRuns the recorder in the background. Stop it with:\n\n```sh\nportlog stop\n```\n\n## Look up a port\n\nSee every process that held port 3000, newest first:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000\n```\n\nLimit the history to the last two hours:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000 --since 2h\n```\n\n## Free a port\n\nStop the process holding port 3000 now:\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000\n```\n\nportlog asks for confirmation first. Add `-y` to skip it.\n\n## How it works\n\nThe recorder polls `lsof` every two seconds on macOS and reads `/proc/net/tcp` on Linux. History stays in `~/.portlog/` for seven days.\n\nCommand-line arguments are never recorded because they can hold secrets.\n\n## About\n\nWritten in Go. MIT licensed.",
